    slug: "utility-energy-solar-decommissioning-requirements",
    title: "What Decommissioning Rules Apply to Utility-Scale Solar Farms?",
    description: "Solar decommissioning requirements: financial assurance and bonding, full removal scope, module recycling, land restoration, and the estimates behind them.",
    h1: "What Decommissioning Rules Apply to Utility-Scale Solar Farms?",
    answer: "Every utility-scale solar farm eventually comes down — and the requirements governing that removal are increasingly set at permitting, not at end of life. Counties and states now routinely require decommissioning plans and financial assurance (bonds, letters of credit, escrow) as conditions of the land-use permit. The engineering behind decommissioning has two products: the removal plan and the cost estimate that sizes the bond.\n\nThe removal scope: modules, racking, piles/foundations, inverters and transformers, collection system cabling (underground cable removal depth is negotiated — full removal vs. abandonment below a depth), substation equipment, buildings, roads, and fencing. Module recycling is the fastest-evolving piece — PV modules contain glass, aluminum, silicon, and small amounts of regulated materials; the recycling industry (and in some states, regulatory requirements like Washington's PV module stewardship program) is maturing to handle end-of-life volumes. Hazardous materials — transformer oil, battery components on paired-storage sites — follow their own disposal regulations.\n\nLand restoration returns the site to its pre-project condition or the permitted after-use: grading to restore drainage, decompaction of construction-disturbed soils, revegetation with the approved seed mix, and removal of below-grade infrastructure to the agreed depth. The cost estimate — prepared by the engineer, typically in current dollars with inflation/escalation provisions and net of salvage value (a negotiated point: counties often discount or disallow salvage) — sets the bond amount, commonly revisited on a 5-year cycle. Our decommissioning estimates are built from quantity takeoffs against the actual design, not per-MW rules of thumb, because the bond amount is negotiated against our numbers. Getting the estimate right at permitting prevents the two failure modes: a bond so large it kills project economics, or one so small the county rejects it.",
    directAnswer: "Decommissioning requirements are set at permitting: a removal plan (modules, racking, foundations, cabling, substation, roads) and financial assurance — bonds or letters of credit sized by an engineer's cost estimate, typically revisited every 5 years. Module recycling follows evolving state programs; land restoration covers grading, decompaction, and revegetation. Takeoff-based estimates prevent bonds that kill economics or get rejected.",
